<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §01 ─── -->
<section id="s-memory" data-code="memory">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Memory Wall</div>
<h2>KV cache is the new memory bottleneck</h2>
<p>
  During autoregressive decoding, every new token must read the entire KV cache — keys and values
  for every prior token, every layer, every head. At BF16 (2 bytes per element), a single
  Qwen3-8B serving a 128K-token context needs roughly <strong>16 GB just for the KV cache</strong>.
  Scale to 100 concurrent requests and you've consumed the entire GPU.
</p>
<p>
  INT2 quantization (2 bits, 8× smaller than BF16) is the theoretical sweet spot: dense, hardware-friendly,
  no per-channel routing metadata. But only 4 quantization levels are available — each wrong bit costs dearly.
  The chart below shows how KV memory scales with context length across precision formats.
</p>

<!-- Memory wall canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">KV cache memory — Qwen3-8B, 32 layers, 8 heads, d=128</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover a bar for GB value</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-width:580px">
    <canvas id="memory-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  INT4 offers a 4× reduction; INT2 offers 8×. The difference is dramatic at long contexts:
  at 128K tokens, INT2 frees enough headroom to serve <em>8× more concurrent requests</em> under the same GPU
  memory budget — or to serve a single user with a 1M-token context window.
  The catch: getting INT2 to actually work is the hard part.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §02 ─── -->
<section id="s-outlier" data-code="outlier">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Outlier Problem</div>
<h2>A few channels hijack the quantization scale</h2>
<p>
  KV activations are not uniformly distributed. A small subset of channels — often tied to
  positional encodings or attention sink tokens — have magnitudes 10–100× larger than the rest.
  When you quantize with a min-max scale, these outliers dominate the range, compressing
  all normal values into just one or two distinct quantization levels.
</p>
<p>
  The standard fix is a <strong>Hadamard rotation</strong>: multiply the activation by an orthogonal Walsh–Hadamard matrix
  <code>H</code> before quantization. This mixes all channels, redistributing the outlier energy uniformly
  — each element picks up a small contribution from every input channel. Click the buttons below to
  compare raw, Hadamard-rotated, and OSCAR-rotated K-activation histograms.
</p>

<!-- Activation distribution interactive canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">K-activation channel magnitudes (one layer, head 0)</span>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem">
      <button class="act-btn active" data-mode="raw">Raw INT2</button>
      <button class="act-btn" data-mode="hadamard">Hadamard</button>
      <button class="act-btn" data-mode="oscar">OSCAR</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-width:580px">
    <canvas id="act-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="act-caption" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);line-height:1.6"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Hadamard rotation <em>does</em> help — the outlier spikes vanish, and peak magnitudes drop significantly.
  But as Figure 2 in the paper shows, Hadamard-only rotation still leaves substantial KL divergence
  in the attention score distribution at INT2. Something else is going wrong.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §03 ─── -->
<section id="s-gap" data-code="gap">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Alignment Gap</div>
<h2>Attention doesn't care about Euclidean distance</h2>
<p>
  Here is the core insight. When you rotate K by some matrix R and quantize, the downstream
  attention error is <em>not</em> the Euclidean reconstruction error <code>‖K − Q(KR)R⊤‖</code>.
  Keys enter attention as <strong>dot products with queries</strong>:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Key logit distortion</div>
  ‖QK⊤ − Q<span class="math-var">K̂</span>⊤‖² ∝ tr(<span class="math-var">E</span>ₖ · <span class="math-var">Q⊤Q</span>)
  <div class="math-comment" style="margin-top:.5rem">
    where Eₖ = per-channel quantization noise — the query covariance Q⊤Q weights which channels matter
  </div>
</div>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Value output distortion</div>
  ‖SV − S<span class="math-var">V̂</span>‖² ∝ tr(<span class="math-var">E</span>ᵥ · <span class="math-var">S⊤S</span>)
  <div class="math-comment" style="margin-top:.5rem">
    where Eᵥ = per-channel noise — the score matrix S⊤S weights which token positions matter
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  A generic Hadamard rotation equalizes channel energy, but it doesn't know which directions
  are <em>important to queries</em>. The optimal rotation should diagonalize the query covariance
  <code>C_Q = (1/N) Σ qₙ⊤qₙ</code> — aligning the low-error quantization axes with the
  directions that queries most strongly project onto. The visualization below contrasts the two.
</p>

<!-- Alignment gap visualization -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">2D key space: rotation target comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to toggle</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-width:580px">
    <canvas id="align-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;cursor:pointer"></canvas>
    <div id="align-caption" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);line-height:1.6;text-align:center"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Key intuition:</strong> INT2 has only 4 levels. Quantization noise is largest in the
  <em>first</em> basis vector (widest range). Hadamard puts the widest direction wherever raw
  variance is largest. OSCAR puts the widest direction wherever <em>queries care least</em> —
  pushing noise into directions the attention dot products barely read.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────── §04 ─── -->
<section id="s-oscar" data-code="oscar">
<div class="section-no">04 — OSCAR Calibration</div>
<h2>Offline covariance → per-layer rotation matrices</h2>
<p>
  OSCAR's offline calibration pass collects a small set of key, query, and value activations
  (8,878 tokens from an MMLU-style dataset — a single forward pass), then builds per-layer
  rotation matrices that minimize the frozen-error surrogates above.
</p>

<h3>The composed rotation</h3>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">OSCAR rotation formula</div>
  <span class="math-var">R</span>ₖ = <span class="math-var">U</span>_Q · <span class="math-var">H</span>_Had · <span class="math-var">P</span>_br
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">R</span>ᵥ = <span class="math-var">U</span>_S · <span class="math-var">H</span>_Had · <span class="math-var">P</span>_br
  <div class="math-comment" style="margin-top:.5rem">
    U_Q = eigenvectors of CQ = (1/N) Σ qₙ⊤qₙ  (key rotation)<br>
    U_S = eigenvectors of CS = (1/N) V⊤S⊤SV  (value rotation)<br>
    H_Had = Hadamard to further equalize diagonal energy<br>
    P_br = bit-reversal permutation to balance high/low variance across INT2 groups
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Theorem 1 in the paper proves that <code>U_Q</code> and <code>U_S</code> are optimal under a
  natural "frozen-error" surrogate: if quantization residuals are diagonal in the ambient basis,
  aligning the rotation with these eigenvectors minimizes the attention distortion.
</p>

