</div>

<p>
  At 128K context, the BF16 windows protect only <strong>0.24% of tokens</strong> (64 sink + 256 recent),
  contributing negligible memory overhead while preserving the highest-impact tokens at full precision.
  The ablation in Table 5 shows a clear knee at (S=64, R=256): smaller windows leave accuracy on the table,
  larger windows add cost without benefit.
</p>

<h3>Fused Triton kernel</h3>
<p>
  The write path fuses rotate → clip → INT2 quantize → pack into a single Triton kernel.
  The read path unpacks 4 values-per-byte, dequantizes, and feeds directly into Flash-style paged attention.
  Critically, OSCAR absorbs R_V into the model's value projection weight (<code>W_V ← W_V · R_V⊤</code>),
  so no rotation happens at inference time for values — the stored INT2 vectors are already in the
  quantization-friendly basis.
</p>

<h3>Throughput gains</h3>
<p>
  Table 8 in the paper shows that OSCAR's attention kernel reduces per-step decode time by roughly 3×
  at 100K context (Qwen3-4B) purely from reduced KV-cache bandwidth. At large batch sizes the
  gains are even larger: 7.83× throughput vs BF16 at BS=32 (Qwen3-8B).
</p>
</section>

